  {
    "file": "retrieval-log/session_2026-09-18-research-batch-15-t2-synthesis-threshold/manifest.jsonl",
    "line": 1,
    "summary": "All 8 retrieval_log.fetch() calls omit the ref_id= argument, so every artefact in the manifest carries ref_id: null and every claim_text verification in the batch resolves to the weak UNSCOPED branch.",
    "failure_scenario": "quote_in_artefacts(quote, ref_id=...) compares rec.get(\"ref_id\") == ref_id to decide between a scoped hit and the UNSCOPED result, whose detail says the match \"proves the words are in the corpus, not that they came from this source\". With ref_id null on all 8 records, a claim_text from REF-00999's PubMed XML would verify identically against REF-00977's artefact. Batches 08 and 09 populated ref_id (8/9 and 3/8 records); batches 10-15 do not, so this is a regression in the CLAUDE.md §5(c) anti-fabrication control, and the attestation's \"Every claim_text on every extraction verifies byte-for-byte against a persisted artefact\" overstates what was actually proved."
  },
  {
    "file": "workplan/2026-09-18-pipeline-content-review-and-walkability.md",
    "line": 110,
    "summary": "The workplan shipped in this PR asserts the opposite of the database shipped in the same PR: it records spec 7 as the live determination while the same commit range retires it.",
    "failure_scenario": "Line 110's table row reads `| 7 | no | — |` (not retired), line 69 says \"Parameter 3 has a live determination — 5 % / 1:20, provisional\", and line 113 says \"no keyed route to spec 7, the live determination\". data_20260918041751_*.sql line 100 retires spec 7 in the same diff. A reader landing on this workplan is told a determination exists that the accompanying DB no longer has — CLAUDE.md §5(b), \"prose that contradicts the database\". The §1 walk table (59/59, 15/16, 26/26, 12/16) is likewise stale against the shipped DB (65/65, 19/20, 33/33, 16/20)."
  },
  {
    "file": "scripts/db.py",
    "line": 5779,
    "summary": "link_source_slug validates only that the slug exists in the slugs table, so it will happily cross-file evidence into a MERGED or STUB slug.",
    "failure_scenario": "`SELECT 1 FROM slugs WHERE slug=?` matches all 106 rows, but slugs.status is CHECK-constrained to ACTIVE/MERGED/STUB/PROVISIONAL and the live distribution is ACTIVE=80, STUB=23, MERGED=3. `link-source-slug --slug <a-merged-slug>` succeeds; the link is then invisible to every reader that scopes to the ACTIVE set (tools/evidentiary_audit.py audits \"the 80 ACTIVE slugs\"), and a MERGED slug carries merged_into pointing at its replacement, so the evidence is filed into a topic that was folded into another. The refusal message even asserts \"The vocabulary is the table's own, never a guess\" — but the filing vocabulary is the ACTIVE subset, not the table."
  },
  {
    "file": "scripts/audit/citation_mining_completeness.py",
    "line": 158,
    "summary": "Introducing multi-slug links breaks the mining-completeness gate's per-slug semantics: the cm join ignores slug, so one mining pass now marks a source mined for every slug it is linked to.",
    "failure_scenario": "citation_mining is keyed per (slug, local_ref_id) and log_mining writes one row per (slug, ref_id), but the outstanding query joins `cm.global_ref_id = es.ref_id` with no slug predicate, and evidence_sources.citation_mining_status is one flag per source. Before this PR every source had exactly one slug, so the two were equivalent; REF-00999 is now linked to 5 slugs and a single forward pass logged under accessible-circulation-geometry satisfies the R2 obligation for all 5. Symmetrically, the cm2 legacy fallback (`cm2.slug = ssl.slug AND cm2.local_ref_id = ssl.local_ref_id`) now fans out 5×, so a legacy-mined source produces 4 rows with cm2.slug NULL and is reported UNMINED — the 48-false-positive shape that join was added to prevent."
  },
  {
    "file": "scripts/db.py",
    "line": 5732,
    "summary": "insert_source_slug_link uses INSERT OR IGNORE while link_source_slug's duplicate pre-check runs on a separate read-only connection, so the command can report a write it did not perform.",
    "failure_scenario": "The `SELECT 1 FROM source_slug_links WHERE ref_id=? AND slug=?` guard closes its connection before the write connection opens. If the row lands in between (or the PK (ref_id, slug) is otherwise hit), the INSERT OR IGNORE is a silent no-op and link_source_slug still returns {\"ref_id\":..., \"slug\":..., \"local_ref_id\": \"22\", \"dry_run\": false}, which _emit prints as success and the command log records as a write. db.py's own add-source comment names this class: \"A writer that merely INSERTs is worse than hand SQL because it looks safe ...; one that REPORTS a write it skipped is worse still.\" The allocated local_ref_id is also consumed and reported for a row that does not exist."
  },
  {
    "file": "scratchpad/batch-13-content-review-kpz7u8/walk.py",
    "line": 34,
    "summary": "The re-derivation script the workplan points readers at is pinned to literal ids 7 and to 4 of the 5 JSON ref-id columns, and crashes on a NULL one.",
    "failure_scenario": "Lines 32-41 hardcode `convergence_id=7` and `specification_id=7`; this PR retires specification 7, so a reader following the workplan's \"Re-derive with scratchpad/batch-13-content-review-kpz7u8/walk.py\" will keep measuring a retired row once a successor with a new id exists, and F3's rule-5 comparison silently describes the dead determination. Line 34 sums clinical_sources, co1_sources, co2_sources and discounted_sources but omits down_weighted_sources, which convergence_assessment also declares as a JSON array of REF-IDs — it is `[]` today so the printed 14 happens to be right, but the first down-weighted source silently undercounts, and the workplan's F2 prose repeats the wrong column list (\"four columns\"). All five columns are nullable, and `json.loads(None)` raises TypeError, so one NULL aborts the whole script."
  },
  {
    "file": "scripts/db.py",
    "line": 5786,
    "summary": "The derived local_ref_id strips every non-digit from existing labels, so a slug using an alphabetic label scheme gets a bare integer that collides with and mismatches the scheme.",
    "failure_scenario": "`int(re.sub(r\"\\D\", \"\", t))` maps \"ACG-08\" → 8, so a slug still on the ACG-NN scheme (which accessible-circulation-geometry itself used until the 2026-09-13 clear — see data_20260902220357_*.sql, ACG-01..ACG-09) yields local_ref_id \"10\" rather than \"ACG-10\", mixing two label schemes in one slug. citation_mining copies that label from source_slug_links and joins on string equality, and source_slug_links has no UNIQUE(slug, local_ref_id) (GAP-285, per scripts/audit/source_slug_links_duplicates.py), so nothing refuses a label that duplicates or mismatches an existing one. Distinct families like \"A-1\" and \"B-1\" both reduce to 1."
  },
  {
    "file": "scripts/db.py",
    "line": 5774,
    "summary": "link_source_slug checks only that the ref_id is present in evidence_sources, not that it is a live row, so a superseded source can be cross-filed into a new slug.",
    "failure_scenario": "`SELECT 1 FROM evidence_sources WHERE ref_id=?` matches rows carrying a non-empty superseded_by_ref_id. db.py's own R9 duplicate check at line 3105 excludes exactly those rows on the stated ground that \"neither row is a live claim\" — that judgement is not applied here, so `link-source-slug --ref-id <a-superseded-ref>` files retired evidence into a fresh topic, where it counts as a source-instance in the evidentiary audit and as an anchoring candidate."
  },
  {
    "file": "scripts/db.py",
    "line": 5747,
    "summary": "The new docstring hard-codes a corpus count with no regenerating command, and the figure is already false by the end of the session that wrote it.",
    "failure_scenario": "\"Measured the day this was written: ALL 16 sources in evidence_sources were linked to exactly ONE slug, and not one had ever been cross-filed.\" By the time this PR merges evidence_sources holds 20 rows and 4 of them span more than one slug. CLAUDE.md rule 7a: \"A NUMBER IS WRITTEN AS THE COMMAND THAT COMPUTES IT, NOT AS ITS RESULT ... Where a document, a comment, a registry entry, a commit message or a criterion needs a count, it carries the command that regenerates it\" — and its own proof is a hand-typed count that \"went stale inside the rule against hand-typed counts, within an hour of being written.\" Nothing goes red when this comment is wrong."
  },
  {
    "file": "scripts/db.py",
    "line": 3116,
    "summary": "The new verb was added but none of the refusals that send an operator to it were swept, so the CLI still names the action without naming the command.",
    "failure_scenario": "db.py:3078, :3116 and :6343, plus research_batch_dod.py:476 and :585, all still read \"R9: cross-file the existing ref_id ... Link that ref_id to your slug instead\" with no mention of `db.py link-source-slug`. The PR's entire stated premise is that \"the instruction named an action the CLI could not perform\"; the operator who hits the R9 refusal tomorrow is given the same unactionable sentence and has no way to discover the verb short of reading --help. CLAUDE.md rule 4 explicitly marks prose callers as the class that no gate sweeps."
